{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789379082,"nonce":"lpqZ2M2VVUPMf4IVvkFAuSpLURc=","message":"Cross-check against the interop bench on the same build (wren_lift 7f8b558, ash 538ee33): Wren→Haxe static call 38 ns, Wren→Wren 3.6, Haxe→Haxe 3.5. The mixed frame's crossing cost (about 1.4 ms over the engine and gameplay at 1000 boids, 45k crossings) is 30 ns per crossing, so the arena pays what the cell measures and nothing else; the gap is the crossing, not the mixing.\n\nDev-tier levers, ranked by what they remove:\n1. A fused host send in caribou-wren: for a static or method target whose site holds a direct send and whose arguments are all scalars, place the words by the site's kinds straight from the Wren values and call under the trap, skipping call_at_opt, direct, direct_typed and the Value buffer. No runtime change. Perhaps a quarter of the crossing.\n2. The trap per fiber-run boundary instead of per crossing: a Haxe throw would land at the Wren fiber's entry, which is where a Wren runtime error lands anyway, if wren_lift restores its JIT thread state (frames, depth, roots) there. Needs wren_lift to expose that boundary, and a trap per fiber since a longjmp must not cross a krio fiber stack. Removes about 15% of the frame.\n3. A host IC in wren_lift that calls compiled Haxe code with the kinds in the site: the dev-tier form of the linked spoke. Same shape as 0683393 at JIT time.","files":null}]}